-- title:
--   Covering radius of the integer lattice
-- statement:
--   For every point $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ there is a lattice point $y \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ with
--
--   $$\lVert y - z \rVert \;\le\; \frac{\sqrt{d}}{2}.$$
--
--   Equivalently, the closed balls of radius $\tfrac12\sqrt d$ centred at the integer lattice cover $\mathbb{R}^d$: rounding each coordinate to a nearest integer moves each coordinate by at most $\tfrac12$, hence moves the point by at most $\tfrac12\sqrt d$ in the Euclidean norm.
--
--   This is the covering-radius bound for $\mathbb{Z}^d$ and the basic mechanism for replacing an arbitrary point of space by a nearby lattice point at a cost of at most $\tfrac12\sqrt{d}$ — the error term that appears in essentially every estimate comparing a continuous statement with its lattice version.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis is placed on $z$ or on $d$; for $d = 0$ the space is trivial and the statement is degenerate but true.
